  "generated_text": "To solve the problem, we start by analyzing the given formula for the number of players given elite status, which is \\(2^{1+\\lfloor \\log_{2} (N-1) \\rfloor} - N\\). We know that this number is equal to 19. So we have:\n\n\\[2^{1+\\lfloor \\log_{2} (N-1) \\rfloor} - N = 19.\\]\n\nFirst, let's denote \\(k = \\lfloor \\log_{2} (N-1) \\rfloor\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\n\\[2^{1+k} - N = 19 \\implies 2^{k+1} - N = 19 \\implies N = 2^{k+1} - 19.\\]\n\nSince \\(k = \\lfloor \\log_{2} (N-1) \\rfloor\\), it follows that:\n\n\\[2^k \\leq N-1 < 2^{k+1}.\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(N = 2^{k+1} - 19\\) into the inequality, we get:\n\n\\[2^k \\leq 2^{k+1} - 20 < 2^{k+1}.\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[2^k \\leq 2^{k+1} - 20 < 2^{k+1}.\\]\n\nThe first part of the inequality \\(2^k \\leq 2^{k+1} - 20\\) is always true because \\(2^{k+1} - 20 > 2^k\\). So we focus on the second part:\n\n\\[2^{k+1} - 20 < 2^{k+1}.\\]\n\nThis is also always true. Now, we need to find the integer \\(k\\) such that \\(N = 2^{k+1} - 19\\) is a valid number of players. This means \\(N-1\\) must be in the range \\([2^k, 2^{k+1})\\), so:\n\n\\[2^k \\leq 2^{k+1} - 20 < 2^{k+1}.\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[2^k \\leq 2^{k+1} - 20 < 2^{k+1}.\\]\n\nWe need to check values of \\(k\\) to find a valid \\(N\\).\n\n1. If \\(k = 4\\), then \\(N = 2^{4+1} - 19 = 32 - 19 = 13\\). We check the range: \\(2^4 \\leq 13-1 < 2^5 \\implies 16 \\leq 12 < 32\\), which is not true.\n2. If \\(k = 5\\), then \\(N = 2^{5+1} - 19 = 64 - 19 = 45\\). We check the range: \\(2^5 \\leq 45-1 < 2^6 \\implies 32 \\leq 44 < 64\\), which is true.\n\nSo, \\(N = 45\\) is a valid solution. We need to check if there is a smaller \\(N\\). Since \\(k = 4\\) does not work, there are no smaller values of \\(k\\) to check. Therefore, the only valid \\(N\\) is 45.\n\nNext, we need to find the next possible value of \\(N\\). We increment \\(k\\) and check the next possible \\(N\\).\n\n3. If \\(k = 6\\), then \\(N = 2^{6+1} - 19 = 128 - 19 = 109\\). We check the range: \\(2^6 \\leq 109-1 < 2^7 \\implies 64 \\leq 108 < 128\\), which is true.\n\nSo, \\(N = 109\\) is another valid solution. The two smallest possible values of \\(N\\) are 45 and 109. The sum of these values is:\n\n\\[45 + 109 = 154.\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{154}.\\]",
